    "text": "**Case A**\n- **A1: Cannot tell.** The seven-day search found only the stable-region requests. That says nothing about use before that window, or about whether the key leaked before publication. It is also not established that the burst was misuse.\n- **A2: Cannot tell.** The burst came before the check ran at 30 minutes. We are not told what data the check could see or when that data reached it.\n- **A3: No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link between the accounts. It does not prove they have one owner.\n- **A4: No.** If someone else controls the payout destination, the account owner may simply not have noticed or reported it. No incident review has been recorded either. Silence here does not show the payouts were legitimate.\n- **A5: Yes.** Several risk signals arrived together soon after publication: a residential proxy, a new fingerprint, a new webhook, a changed payout destination already shared with other accounts, and a faster payout schedule.\n\n**Case B**\n- **B1: Yes.** D has 0 chargebacks in 100 transactions; the others have 2 in 100.\n- **B2: No.** A 5 percent average across the four accounts can hide one account above 5 percent.\n- **B3: Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found only A and the closed account. The rejected application is not an account. (This holds only for exact identity matches; aliases would not show up.)\n- **B4: Cannot tell.** Different analysts and days do not show that the notes rest on independent evidence. Both may have used the same data.\n- **B5: No.** A blocked payment never settled, so it could not get a chargeback. Having no chargeback does not make it a non-fraud transaction.\n- **B6: Yes.** Shared templates, a shared descriptor pattern and common settlement through A to D are enough to justify a joint review.\n\nRESULT CO-050 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: Cannot tell | A3: No, not by itself | A4: No | A5: Yes | B1: Yes | B2: No | B3: Yes | B4: Cannot tell | B5: No | B6: Yes"
